cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "本ベンチマークを利用した場合は、以下を引用してください。 / If you use this benchmark, please cite it as below."
type: software

title: "bim-bench: AIは BIM モデルの空間構造をどこまで読めるか"

abstract: >-
  A benchmark measuring how far large language models can read IFC: the spatial hierarchy, which
  elements belong to which space, the quantities and property sets attached to them, planted defects,
  and — in the latest task — corpus-wide counts under precisely stated counting rules. Two arms differ
  only in tooling: arm B may read files but not execute code; arm C may write its own parser but may
  not use any IFC library. The corpus is buildingSMART's Sample-Test-Files (CC BY 4.0).

  v1.1.0 retracts the headline finding of v1.0.0. That version reported that the code-executing arm was
  "better on every axis". Repeated measurement does not support it. Across five tasks the two arms reach
  the same accuracy whenever the measurement itself holds up; what separates them is cost.

  The retraction has a second, sharper part. Task T004 appeared to separate the arms by 35.7 points.
  External review showed its grader awarded a perfect score to an answer written without opening a single
  file, and that the grader and task text had both been rewritten after the answers were read — the
  revision moved the pilot scores by more than the arm gap it was said to reveal. T004 is therefore
  reported as a failed measurement, not as a result.

  T005 rebuilds the task so those failure modes cannot recur: the counting questions are named by the
  author rather than elicited, so no answer-to-reference matching is needed; no entity identifiers are
  submitted, so padding cannot help; scoring is exact-match only; and an information-free baseline is a
  standing adversarial case (it scores 1.6/100 against the reference solution's 100.0). Task, reference,
  grader and adversarial suite were committed before the arms ran and were not touched afterwards, even
  when the runs exposed a genuine ambiguity in two of the eleven questions.

  Result on T005, three runs per arm: both arms reach 11/11. Every run that lost points lost the same
  two questions, and every such run gave the same alternative answer — an interpretive fork in the
  author's task text, which five of six runs flagged independently. Cost, by contrast, differs by at
  least a factor of 19.6, and that figure is a lower bound because arm B's subagent usage is only
  partially recoverable.

  Known limitation: the two arms were not run under identical conditions. Arm B's session lacked the
  read-only search tools its prompt promised, so it paged roughly 2.5M tokens with a plain file reader.
  It still reached 11/11.

  IFC の読解を測るベンチマーク。v1.0.0 の主要結論「コード実行を許した腕が全ての軸で上回った」を撤回する。
  測定が成立している限り到達範囲では分離せず、分かれるのはコストだけだった。
  35.7点差を示したとされた課題は、何も読まない答案に満点を出す採点器によるもので、失敗した測定として報告する。

